<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  The AI loop: an OUTER machine that drives an INNER agent session.

  The inner session is a pane sprag opened (`open_pane`) running an agent CLI.
  This machine never talks to that agent; it decides WHEN the driver should
  prompt it, when to stop, and when the session itself has to be replaced. The
  driver (sprag-plugin) owns every effect — assembling prompt text, reading the
  pane, editing files, closing and reopening the pane.

  Why an inner session is REPLACED rather than reconfigured: the things a loop
  wants to improve about itself — the agent's base context, which MCP servers
  are loaded, CLAUDE.md, the memory index — are all read when a session starts.
  A live session cannot be asked to re-read them. So `reflecting` writes the
  improvements to disk and `restarting` closes the pane and opens a fresh one,
  which reads them on the way up. That is the whole reason this machine has a
  restart state instead of a "reconfigure" event.

  The terminal outcomes are the Driver's (`orchestration.scxml`) on purpose: a
  loop that ends reports the same shapes any plugin run reports, so one reader
  understands both.

  Where this machine DIFFERS from the Driver is `blocked`, and the difference is
  the whole reason a person can supervise a run. In the Driver a blocked peer
  ends the run, because nobody is watching. Here the inner session is a pane on
  a person's screen: they can read every turn as it happens and type into it
  directly. So an agent that stops to ASK sends this machine to `awaiting_human`
  — it stops prompting and waits. If the person answers the dialog, the agent
  goes back to work and the loop resumes where it was. `blocked` stays as the
  terminal for the case that actually ends a run: nobody came.

  The loop still never answers a dialog itself. That was never about whether a
  human is present — it is about whose decision a tool permission is. Waiting is
  what changes; answering is still not this machine's to do.

  datamodel="ecmascript" (not the `null` its two siblings use) because a loop's
  prompts and goals ARE its definition: the whole point is that a person edits
  them. Its siblings hold pure topology and keep their data in Rust beside the
  engine; here the data is the part being authored, so it lives in the document
  the author edits.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT "ecmascript" IS EVALUATED BY, MEASURED. At the pinned SCE rev there
  is exactly one IScriptEngine — LuaEngine; sce-rust-runtime's own manifest
  calls QuickJS "future". SCE's build special-cases only the datamodel string
  "null" and routes every other value to whatever engine the consumer supplies,
  with no diagnostic. So every expression below is evaluated as LUA, and the
  generated init strings show the codegen rewriting some of the syntax on the
  way in (`[...]` -> `{...}`, `key:` -> `key =`) and not other parts of it
  (`'a' + b` is passed through, where Lua `+` is arithmetic).

  Reading that, this round predicted the composed prompts would fail to
  initialise. THEY DO NOT: `ai_loop.rs`'s gate drives a real LuaEngine and every
  shape here crosses whole — the bare literal, the `+`-composed prompt, the list
  of objects, the scalar. The prediction was wrong and the gate is what said so.

  What that measurement does NOT license is assuming ECMAScript semantics.
  Nothing here has been shown to work beyond the four shapes the gate drives, so
  before writing `===`, `JSON.stringify`, `Array.prototype.map`, `undefined` or a
  template literal into this file, ADD IT TO THAT GATE FIRST. The engine will
  answer; the name at the top of this document will not.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="ai_loop"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="idle">

  <!--
    Everything above the line is the template's editable surface. A person edits
    it here; a caller that did not write the file fills the same parts in through
    the `brief` event on `idle`. Nothing below the line changes per loop.

    ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS ARE DATA AND THE PROMPTS ARE COMPOSED FROM THEM IN `priming`,
    which is the one arrangement that lets both authors work. Composing in
    `<data>` — where this document did it until now — happens exactly once, at
    `initialize()`, so a caller filling a part in afterwards left the composed
    prompt stale and there was no way to reach it: MEASURED, the only prompt any
    caller could make this loop send was

        North star: (edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach
        Milestone: (edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there
        Reference: (edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult

    — three of the five clauses a live agent reads. Writing the composition in
    BOTH places would keep the file-editing author working and is what this
    document already warns against two comments down: two copies of one rule,
    and the failure of letting them drift is silent. `priming` is where it runs
    instead, once, because it is entered on the way to EVERY prompt this loop
    ever sends — from `idle` at the start, and from `restarting` after a
    reflection has rewritten the parts.
  -->
  <datamodel>
    <!-- Where this loop is ultimately going. Never rewritten by reflection. -->
    <data id="north_star" expr="'(edit me) the outcome this loop exists to reach'"/>
    <!-- The step being worked on now. Reflection may rewrite this. -->
    <data id="milestone" expr="'(edit me) the next checkpoint on the way there'"/>
    <!-- Prior art the agent should read before deciding anything. -->
    <data id="reference" expr="'(edit me) paths, URLs or repos to consult'"/>
    <!--
      The agent the driver launches in the inner pane. INTERACTIVE, never
      `-p`/print mode: sprag reads an agent's state from its TUI — the title's
      leading glyph separates working from idle, and a blocked agent is
      recognised by the shape of its dialog. Print mode renders none of that,
      so a `-p` inner session is one this loop cannot observe and a person
      cannot answer.
    -->
    <data id="model" expr="'claude'"/>

    <!--
      Termination: the loop converges when the agent says this.

      ⚠⚠⚠ DECLARED BEFORE THE PROMPTS, and that is load-bearing rather than
      tidy: `<data>` is evaluated in document order, and every working prompt
      below COMPOSES this string. Move it back down and the prompts initialise
      against a marker that does not exist yet.

      ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE IT RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379 measured
      what the alternative costs. The marker existed here, `judging` waited for
      it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED THE
      AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word the
      whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could say
      it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
      spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
      budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
      eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
      to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
      for.

      Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
      it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
      step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.
    -->
    <data id="done_marker" expr="'MILESTONE REACHED'"/>

    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE THREE COMPOSED PROMPTS ARE DECLARED EMPTY HERE AND WRITTEN IN
      `priming`. Their text is up there, in one place, where a person editing
      this template reads it; declaring them is what makes them assignable, and
      what lets a consumer ask whether this machine is one it can drive at all.

      A loop sitting in `idle` therefore holds three empty strings, and that is
      the honest answer rather than a gap: nothing has been primed, so nothing
      has been composed. What a caller can read before then is the PARTS, which
      are what it filled in.
    -->
    <data id="done_instruction" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent once, into a freshly opened session. -->
    <data id="start_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!-- Sent on every turn after the first. -->
    <data id="turn_prompt" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      Sent once before the loop reports converged. NOT composed — it names
      nothing else in this datamodel — so it stays a plain literal, and a
      reader can tell the two kinds apart by whether `priming` writes them.
    -->
    <data id="end_prompt"
          expr="'Summarise what changed, what was verified, and what is left open.'"/>

    <!--
      Standing instructions for dialogs the person has already decided about.
      When the inner agent raises a question, `screening` matches it against
      these before anybody is woken; a match is answered with the reply written
      here and the run carries on.

      This is not the loop deciding. It is the loop CARRYING OUT a decision the
      person made in advance and wrote down — which is why the rules live in the
      authored half of this document and not in the driver. A rule with no match
      wakes the person, exactly as before.

      Each rule is `when` (what shape of dialog it claims) and `reply` (the keys
      and text to send). `keys` is sent first, then `text`.
    -->
    <data id="screen_rules"
          expr="[
            { when: 'design-decision',
              keys: 'Escape',
              text: '비용 무시하고 가장 장기적으로 올바른 방법으로 다시 생각하고 진행' },
            { when: 'design-proposal',
              keys: 'Escape',
              text: '비용 무시하고 가장 장기적으로 올바른 방법으로 다시 생각하고 진행' },
            { when: 'multiple-choice',
              keys: 'Escape',
              text: '비용 무시하고 가장 장기적으로 올바른 방법으로 다시 생각하고 진행' }
          ]"/>

    <!--
      ⚠ Tool-permission dialogs are NOT screened by default, and the flag is
      separate from the rules on purpose.

      Redirecting a design question costs a turn. Approving a permission grants
      an agent the ability to act — delete files, push, spend money — and a
      standing "yes" to that is a different kind of promise than a standing
      "think about it again". Someone who wants it can turn it on knowingly;
      nobody gets it by writing a rule that happened to match.
    -->
    <data id="screen_permissions" expr="false"/>

    <!-- Budgets. `max_turns` bounds the run; `reflect_every` sets how often
         the loop stops to improve itself. -->
    <data id="max_turns" expr="40"/>
    <data id="reflect_every" expr="8"/>

    <!-- ── below the line: loop bookkeeping, not authored ── -->
    <data id="turns" expr="0"/>
    <data id="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
    <!-- How many dialogs a standing instruction answered. Counted so an
         auto-answered run does not read like a hand-answered one. -->
    <data id="screened" expr="0"/>
  </datamodel>

  <state id="idle">
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE TEMPLATE, FILLED IN BY SOMEBODY WHO DID NOT EDIT THIS FILE.

      A person authoring a loop edits the `<data>` above. A GUI — or any caller
      that starts a run — has no file to edit, and until this transition existed
      it had no other way either: the composed prompts were built at
      `initialize()`, so writing a part afterwards left them stale, and the only
      prompt a caller could actually send was the `(edit me)` template.

      TARGETLESS ON PURPOSE. W3C SCXML 3.13: a transition with no `target`
      executes its content and changes no state, so a brief does not exit and
      re-enter `idle` — there is nothing to re-run here, because the composition
      lives in `priming` and every path to a prompt goes through it.

      ⚠ ONLY BEFORE THE RUN STARTS, and that is the whole reason it sits on
      `idle` rather than on the machine. A loop already driving an agent adopts
      new parts through `reflecting` — which writes them, restarts the session,
      and passes back through `priming` — because changing what a run is for
      underneath a working agent is a session replacement, not an assignment.
    -->
    <transition event="brief">
      <assign location="north_star" expr="_event.data.north_star"/>
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
      <assign location="max_turns" expr="_event.data.max_turns"/>
      <assign location="reflect_every" expr="_event.data.reflect_every"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="start" target="priming"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠ A LOOP CAN BE UNSTARTABLE BEFORE IT EVER PROMPTS, and until this edge
      existed `idle` was the one non-final state with no way out but forward.
      What made it concrete: a brief whose text the script engine could not
      carry is already assigned by the time anybody can tell, so the run must
      not be startable — and with no `fail` here the driver's refusal could be
      walked straight past by pumping.
    -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A session exists and has not been prompted yet. Entered at the very
    beginning and again after every restart, which is what makes a restarted
    loop resume with the CURRENT prompts rather than the ones it started with.
  -->
  <state id="priming">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        ⚠⚠⚠ THE COMPOSITION, WRITTEN ONCE AND RUN ON EVERY PATH TO A PROMPT.

        `priming` is entered from `idle` at the start and from `restarting`
        after every session replacement, and those are the only two ways this
        loop ever comes to send a prompt. So composing here is composing exactly
        as often as the parts can have changed — a brief before the run, a
        reflection during it — with one copy of the text.

        ⚠ ORDER IS LOAD-BEARING, as it was in `<data>` before: `done_instruction`
        is composed FIRST because the two working prompts end with it. Executable
        content runs in document order, so moving it below them would append the
        PREVIOUS entry's instruction — silently, and correctly for every entry
        but the first.

        ⚠⚠⚠ AND THE PROMPTS COMPOSE `done_marker` RATHER THAN REPEATING IT. R379
        measured what the alternative costs. The marker existed, `judging` waited
        for it, the driver looked for it on the screen — and NO PROMPT EVER ASKED
        THE AGENT FOR IT. Nothing in the loop was wrong except that the one word
        the whole termination rests on was never said to the only party who could
        say it back. A run against a real agent could not converge; it could only
        spend all `max_turns` and report `exhausted`, which is the word for *the
        budget ran out* and not for *nobody ever asked*. It went unnoticed for
        eleven rounds because the stand-in agent in the driver's gate was written
        to say the marker, which is a fixture manufacturing the answer it tests
        for.

        Composing means an author who edits the marker edits the instruction with
        it. Retyping the word into each prompt would be three places to keep in
        step, and the failure of doing that badly is silent.

        How the agent is told to end a turn it believes finished the milestone is
        prose a person may want in their own language, which is why it is here
        and not in the driver — the driver must not be the thing that appends
        instructions to an authored prompt (that is a second author).
      -->
      <assign location="done_instruction"
              expr="'When the milestone is fully reached AND verified, make the last line of your ' +
                    'reply exactly: ' + done_marker"/>
      <assign location="start_prompt"
              expr="'North star: ' + north_star + '\n' +
                    'Milestone: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    'Reference: ' + reference + '\n' +
                    'Report what you did and what is left.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <assign location="turn_prompt"
              expr="'Continue toward: ' + milestone + '\n' +
                    'Do the next smallest thing that is verifiable, then report.' + '\n' +
                    done_instruction"/>
      <send event="prompt.start"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="prompt.sent" target="working"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The inner agent is running. The driver watches its pane. -->
  <state id="working">
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      The agent asked. Try the person's standing instructions before waking
      them; `screening` is what decides whether this is a question they already
      answered.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>
    <!--
      The person reached into the inner pane and interrupted the agent
      themselves (Escape, Ctrl-C, or just typing). They are plainly here and
      doing something, so the loop stops driving and waits — screening would be
      answering a question the person is already handling.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!-- A watching person can halt the loop between turns. -->
    <transition event="hold" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    A dialog is up. Does a standing instruction cover it?

    A state rather than something the driver swallows, because a rule that fires
    is a thing that happened to the run: it consumed a turn, it steered the
    agent, and whoever reads the trace afterwards needs to see it. A screening
    step hidden inside the driver would make an auto-answered run and a
    hand-answered one look identical.
  -->
  <state id="screening">
    <onentry>
      <assign location="screened" expr="screened + 1"/>
      <send event="screen.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- A rule claimed it; the driver sent the keys and the reply. -->
    <transition event="screen.matched" target="working"/>
    <!-- Nothing covers this one. The person decides. -->
    <transition event="screen.none" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    The run is paused and a person is expected. Two ways in: the agent raised a
    dialog it cannot pass without an answer, or somebody watching asked the loop
    to stop and let them look.

    Nothing is sent from here. The pane stays exactly as the person found it,
    which is what makes it readable — a loop that kept prompting while somebody
    was reading would move the screen out from under them.
  -->
  <state id="awaiting_human">
    <onentry>
      <!--
        The outer loop's own channel to the person. The driver raises a pane
        attention notification, which sprag surfaces in `list_panes` — so a
        waiting run is visible without anybody watching the screen for it.
      -->
      <send event="notify.human"/>
    </onentry>
    <!-- The person answered (or typed a turn themselves) and it completed. -->
    <transition event="turn.done" target="judging">
      <assign location="turns" expr="turns + 1"/>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="turns_since_reflect + 1"/>
    </transition>
    <!--
      The person interrupted again while the loop was already waiting — they are
      working in the pane by hand. Stay put; do not start prompting underneath
      somebody who is typing.
    -->
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <!-- The person looked and waved it on without touching anything. -->
    <transition event="resume" target="working">
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <!-- Nobody came within the driver's patience. This is the run-ending one. -->
    <transition event="unattended" target="blocked"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    One turn has landed. Decide in priority order: goal met, budget spent,
    time to improve ourselves, otherwise take another turn.
  -->
  <state id="judging">
    <transition event="judge" cond="_event.data.done" target="closing"/>
    <transition event="judge" cond="turns &gt;= max_turns" target="exhausted"/>
    <transition event="judge" cond="turns_since_reflect &gt;= reflect_every" target="reflecting"/>
    <transition event="judge" target="working">
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Improve the loop's own setup from the record so far: the prompts, the
    agent's base context, which MCP servers are worth loading, CLAUDE.md, the
    memory index. Every one of those is a FILE the driver writes; none of them
    takes effect until a session reads it, which is why this state's only exit
    is a restart.
  -->
  <state id="reflecting">
    <onentry>
      <assign location="turns_since_reflect" expr="0"/>
      <send event="reflect.begin"/>
    </onentry>
    <!--
      The driver rewrote the loop's parts and/or its environment; adopt them.

      ⚠⚠⚠ THE PARTS, NOT THE PROMPTS, and the change is forced rather than
      tidy. This transition used to assign `start_prompt` and `turn_prompt`
      whole, from text the driver had composed — a second author for the one
      thing this template exists to own. Now that `priming` composes, a prompt
      assigned here would be overwritten on the way through it, which is a
      document that contradicts itself. Handing over the PARTS is the shape that
      survives: `restarting -> priming` recomposes from them, so a reflection
      changes what the loop is aiming at and the template still decides how that
      is said.

      ⚠ `north_star` is deliberately absent. It is where the loop is ultimately
      going, and a run that may rewrite its own destination cannot be said to
      have reached it.
    -->
    <transition event="reflect.applied" target="restarting">
      <assign location="milestone" expr="_event.data.milestone"/>
      <assign location="reference" expr="_event.data.reference"/>
    </transition>
    <!-- Nothing worth changing: carry on without paying for a restart. -->
    <transition event="reflect.none" target="working">
      <send event="prompt.turn"/>
    </transition>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!--
    Close the inner pane and open a fresh one. The new session reads whatever
    `reflecting` wrote on its way up.
  -->
  <state id="restarting">
    <onentry>
      <send event="session.replace"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="session.ready" target="priming"/>
    <!-- A session that will not come back is a failed run, not a stuck one. -->
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
    <transition event="cancel" target="cancelled"/>
  </state>

  <!-- The goal was met; ask for the closing report before ending. -->
  <state id="closing">
    <onentry>
      <send event="prompt.end"/>
    </onentry>
    <transition event="turn.done" target="converged"/>
    <transition event="turn.blocked" target="screening"/>
    <transition event="turn.interrupted" target="awaiting_human"/>
    <transition event="fail" target="failed"/>
  </state>

  <final id="converged"/>
  <final id="exhausted"/>
  <final id="failed"/>
  <final id="cancelled"/>
  <final id="blocked"/>
</scxml>
